1	const USAGE: &str = "
2	Usage: sieve bench
3	       sieve --help
4	
5	Sieve of Eratosthenes
6	
7	A sieve finds prime numbers by first assuming all candidates are prime,
8	then progressively using small primes to mark their multiples composite.
9	
10	Note that a given prime p only needs to start marking its multiples >=p²,
11	as anything less will already have been marked by an even smaller prime
12	factor.  This also means that sieving up to N only requires primes up to
13	sqrt(N) - a useful fact to prepare for parallel sieving.
14	
15	Here we have three forms of the sieve:
16	
17	- `sieve_serial`: direct iteration in serial
18	- `sieve_chunks`: chunked iteration, still serial
19	- `sieve_parallel`: chunked iteration in parallel
20	
21	The performance improvement of `sieve_chunks` is largely due to cache
22	locality.  Since the sieve has to sweep repeatedly over the same memory,
23	it's helpful that the chunk size can fit entirely in the CPU cache.  Then
24	`sieve_parallel` also benefits from this as long as there's cache room for
25	multiple chunks, for the separate jobs in each thread.
26	
27	Commands:
28	    bench              Run the benchmark in different modes and print the timings.
29	
30	Options:
31	    -h, --help         Show this message.
32	";
33	
